# Meavo Release Process

This rule is **identical in every meavo-booths repo** — it has no repo-specific
placeholders. Copy it verbatim; do not customise it per repo. Canonical version:
[meavo-agent-templates/RELEASE_PROCESS.md](https://github.com/meavo-booths/meavo-agent-templates/blob/main/RELEASE_PROCESS.md).

All Meavo repos use `main` (production) and `staging` (integration). Both are
protected: direct pushes are rejected, and a passing `Typecheck` check plus a
pull request are required. Reviews are not required — CI is the gate.

## Never push to main or staging

```bash
# ❌ BAD — rejected by branch protection
git checkout main && git commit -m "..." && git push

# ✅ GOOD — branch off staging, then open a PR
git fetch origin
git checkout -b feat/my-change origin/staging
git commit -m "..."
git push -u origin feat/my-change
gh pr create --base staging
```

## The flow

1. Branch off `staging` using a `feat/`, `fix/`, or `chore/` prefix.
2. Open a PR into `staging`. CI runs and Vercel builds a preview deployment.
3. Merge with **squash** once green. Verify on the staging URL:
   `https://<project>-git-staging-meavo-gateway.vercel.app`
4. To release, open a PR from `staging` into `main` and merge with a
   **merge commit**. Merging to `main` deploys production.

Never squash `staging` into `main` — it rewrites the commits and leaves the two
branches permanently diverged.

## Deploying

Pushing to `main` is what deploys production, and it only happens by merging a
release PR. Never run `vercel --prod`.

## Shared database

Every app reads one Neon database. `DATABASE_URL` is scoped so that **every**
preview deployment — `staging` and every `feat/` branch alike — resolves the
`staging` Neon branch, and only `production` resolves production data. Preview and
production are separate variable records; never widen the production record back
to `preview`.

Cross-app URLs (`AUTH_URL`, `GATEWAY_URL`, `HOLS_SYNC_URL`) are scoped to the
`staging` branch alone, so sign-in on a `feat/` preview can misbehave. Use the
staging URL for anything involving auth.

`@meavo/db` is pinned per app by git tag, so schema changes must stay backward
compatible — add columns rather than renaming or dropping them, since older apps
still read the same tables.

## Changing environment variables

Vercel resolves environment variables when a deployment is built, so editing a
variable does nothing to deployments that already exist. Redeploy afterwards, or
the change silently has no effect:

```bash
vercel redeploy https://<project>-git-staging-meavo-gateway.vercel.app \
  --scope meavo-gateway
```
